In a hypotonic saline solution, the extracellular fluid has a lower concentration of dissolved salts (solutes) than the intracellular fluid of the cell. Water moves across the semipermeable plasma membrane by osmosis from the area of higher water potential (outside) to the area of lower water potential (inside). Consequently, **water enters the cell**, causing it to swell and potentially burst if the influx is excessive.

**Correct choice:** B  

\text{{B}}
